            "text": "An efficient implementation in Python to compute the sum of Euclidean distances over all ordered pairs.\n\nTo prevent timeouts and manage memory efficiently, the computation converts the float32 vectors to float64 to ensure numerical precision and uses BLAS-accelerated matrix multiplication (`GEMM`) in chunks. This computes the pairwise squared distances $\\|u - v\\|^2 = \\|u\\|^2 + \\|v\\|^2 - 2\\langle u, v \\rangle$ without materializing large 3D difference tensors or incurring high memory bus overhead.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n\ndef main():\n    # Load vectors and cast to float64 for numerical precision\n    X = np.load(\"vectors_secondary.npy\").astype(np.float64)\n    N = X.shape[0]\n\n    # Precompute squared L2 norms of each row\n    norms = np.sum(X**2, axis=1)\n\n    total = 0.0\n    chunk_size = 2000\n\n    for start in range(0, N, chunk_size):\n        end = min(start + chunk_size, N)\n        X_chunk = X[start:end]\n\n        # Use GEMM for fast pairwise inner products\n        dot = X_chunk @ X.T\n\n        # Compute squared Euclidean distances: ||u - v||^2 = ||u||^2 + ||v||^2 - 2<u, v>\n        d2 = norms[start:end, None] + norms[None, :] - 2.0 * dot\n\n        # Clamp small negative values caused by floating-point rounding\n        np.maximum(d2, 0.0, out=d2)\n\n        # Zero out the exact diagonal for this chunk\n        np.fill_diagonal(d2[:, start:end], 0.0)\n\n        # Compute Euclidean distance and accumulate sum\n        d = np.sqrt(d2, out=d2)\n        total += float(np.sum(d))\n\n    print(f\"TOTAL:{total}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
